<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p><m>\mathbf{c} = {\left(\begin{array}{c} 2 \\ -5 \end{array}\right)}</m>. Give two vectors <m>\mathbf{p}</m> and <m>\mathbf{q}</m> with <m>\mathbf{p} + \mathbf{q} = \mathbf{c}</m>. Neither <m>\mathbf{p}</m> nor <m>\mathbf{q}</m> may be horizontal or vertical: each must have two non-zero components. (There are many correct answers.)</p><div><m>\mathbf{p} =</m> <fillin characters="3" name="ans1"/>  <m>\quad \mathbf{q} =</m> <fillin characters="3" name="ans2"/> </div><div/></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Split each component of <m>\mathbf{c}</m> into two non-zero parts. For example, take <m>\mathbf{q} = {\left(\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ 1 \end{array}\right)}</m>; then <m>\mathbf{p} = \mathbf{c} - \mathbf{q} = {\left(\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ -6 \end{array}\right)}</m>. Check: <m>{\left(\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ -6 \end{array}\right)} + {\left(\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ 1 \end{array}\right)} = {\left(\begin{array}{c} 2 \\ -5 \end{array}\right)}</m>.</p><p>Drawn head to tail, <m>\mathbf{p}</m> then <m>\mathbf{q}</m> ends where <m>\mathbf{c}</m> ends.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\left(\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ -6 \end{array}\right)</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\left(\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ 1 \end{array}\right)</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>